WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Transcriber: TED Translators Admin
Reviewer: Rhonda Jacobs

00:00:13.182 --> 00:00:18.755
For too long, those of us
who live in cities big and small

00:00:18.779 --> 00:00:21.846
have accepted the unacceptable.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:27.211
We accept that in cities
our sense of time is warped,

00:00:27.235 --> 00:00:31.156
because we have to waste so much of it

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:35.955
just adapting to the absurd organization
and long distances

00:00:35.979 --> 00:00:38.903
of most of today's cities.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:43.356
Why is it we who have to adapt

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:47.020
and to degrade our potential
quality of life?

00:00:47.792 --> 00:00:52.866
Why is it not the city
that responds to our needs?

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:59.850
Why have we left cities
to develop on the wrong path for so long?

00:01:00.648 --> 00:01:03.636
I would like to offer a concept of cities

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:08.713
that goes in the opposite direction
to modern urbanism,

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:15.116
an attempt at converging life
into a human-sized space

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:20.566
rather than fracturing it
into inhuman bigness

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:22.886
and then forcing us to adapt.

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:27.262
I call it "the 15-minute city."

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.586
And in a nutshell,

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:35.184
the idea is that cities
should be designed or redesigned

00:01:35.208 --> 00:01:40.526
so that within the distance
of a 15-minute walk

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:42.216
or bike ride,

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.326
people should be able to live

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:49.706
the essence of what constitutes
the urban experience:

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:52.266
to access work,

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:53.496
housing,

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.246
food, health, education,

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.423
culture and leisure.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.996
Have you ever stopped to ask yourself:

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:10.846
Why does a noisy and polluted street
need to be a noisy and polluted street?

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:12.429
Just because it is?

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:17.776
Why can't it be a garden street
lined with trees,

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:21.626
where people can actually meet
and walk to the baker

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.981
and kids can walk to school?

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:30.016
Our acceptance of the dysfunctions
and indignities of modern cities

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.326
has reached a peak.

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:33.696
We need to change that.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.746
We need to change it
for the sake of justice,

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.613
of our well-being

00:02:39.637 --> 00:02:41.793
and of the climate.

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:46.123
What do we need
to create 15-minute cities?

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:52.800
First, we need to start asking questions
that we have forgotten.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:59.937
For instance, we need to look hard
at how we use our square meters.

00:02:59.961 --> 00:03:01.979
What is that space for?

00:03:02.003 --> 00:03:04.632
Who's using it and how?

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:08.186
We need to understand
what resources we have

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:11.053
and how they are used.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:18.416
Then we need to ask what services
are available in the vicinity --

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:21.246
not only in the city center,

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.653
in every vicinity.

00:03:23.677 --> 00:03:28.046
Health providers, shops,
artisans, markets,

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:30.326
sports, cultural life,

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:32.836
schools, parks.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:34.456
Are there green areas?

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.275
Are there water fountains
placed to cool off

00:03:37.299 --> 00:03:39.311
during the frequent heat waves?

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.386
We also have to ask ourselves:

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:43.596
How do we work?

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:49.870
Why is the place I live here,
and work is far away?

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:55.716
We need to rethink cities
around the four guiding principles

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:00.259
that are the key building blocks
of the 15-minute city.

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:05.502
First, ecology: for a green
and sustainable city.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:08.826
Second, proximity:

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:13.629
to live with reduced distance
to other activities.

00:04:14.097 --> 00:04:18.818
Third, solidarity:
to create links between people.

00:04:20.167 --> 00:04:25.836
Finally, participation
should actively involve citizens

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:29.311
in the transformation
of their neighborhood.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:31.816
Don't get me wrong --

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:35.573
I'm not angling for cities
to become rural hamlets.

00:04:36.601 --> 00:04:40.289
Urban life is vibrant and creative.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:46.890
Cities are places of economic
dynamism and innovation.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:52.455
But we need to make urban life
more pleasant, agile,

00:04:52.479 --> 00:04:54.719
healthy and flexible.

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:59.386
To do so, we need to make sure everyone --

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:01.486
and I mean everyone,

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:05.776
those living downtown
and those living at the fringes --

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:11.596
has access to all key services
within proximity.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:13.400
How do we get this done?

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:19.656
The first city to adopt
the 15-minute city idea

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.086
is Paris,

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:22.269
France.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:28.536
Mayor Anne Hidalgo has suggested
a big bang of proximity,

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.786
which includes, for instance,
a massive decentralization,

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:36.787
developing new services
for each of the districts --

00:05:36.811 --> 00:05:38.682
(City sounds)

00:05:38.706 --> 00:05:44.005
a reduction of traffic by increasing
bike lanes into spaces of leisure;

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:49.616
new economic models
to encourage local shops;

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:51.892
building more green spaces;

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:56.766
transform existing infrastructure,

00:05:56.790 --> 00:06:01.226
for instance, fabrication labs
in sports centers

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:06.946
or turning schools into neighborhood
centers in the evenings.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:12.860
That's actually a golden rule
of the 15-minute city:

00:06:12.884 --> 00:06:19.615
every square meter that’s already built
should be used for different things.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:27.032
The 15-minute city is an attempt
to reconcile the city

00:06:27.056 --> 00:06:29.596
with the humans that live in it.

00:06:30.328 --> 00:06:35.237
The 15-minute city
should have three key features.

00:06:36.181 --> 00:06:42.032
First, the rhythm of the city
should follow humans, not cars.

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:49.640
Second, each square meter
should serve many different purposes.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:55.046
Finally, neighborhoods should be designed

00:06:55.070 --> 00:07:01.076
so that we can live,
work and thrive in them

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:04.630
without having to constantly
commute elsewhere.

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:08.016
It's funny if you think of it:

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:11.291
the way many modern cities are designed

00:07:11.315 --> 00:07:16.246
is often determined
by the imperative to save time,

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:20.506
and yet so much time is lost to commuting,

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:22.951
sitting in traffic jams,

00:07:22.975 --> 00:07:24.936
driving to a mall,

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:29.494
in a bubble of illusory acceleration.

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:35.766
The 15-minute city idea
answers the question of saving time

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:38.491
by turning it on its head,

00:07:39.454 --> 00:07:43.770
by suggesting a different pace of life.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.281
A 15-minute pace.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:49.953
Thank you.

